    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2731.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 738,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to understand water truly, you have to look at it through a few different lenses: its physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The \"Weird\" Science of Water)\nWater is one of the most unique and unusual substances in the universe. While most substances contract when they freeze and expand when they heat up, water defies the norm:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (rivers, oceans), **solid** (ice, snow), and **gas** (steam, water vapor).\n* **Ice Floats:** When water freezes, its molecules arrange themselves in a crystalline structure that takes up *more* space than liquid water. This makes ice less dense than liquid water, which is why ice cubes float. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **The Universal Solvent:** Water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals). Because of this, it acts as nature’s transport system, carrying chemicals, nutrients, and minerals wherever it goes.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why coastal areas have milder climates than inland areas, and it’s why our bodies (which are mostly water) don’t overheat instantly.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater is the defining feature of our home planet, often called the \"Blue Planet.\"\n* **Abundance:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly by the oceans.\n* **The Catch:** Roughly **96.5% of Earth's water is salt water** in the oceans, which humans and land animals cannot drink. \n* **Freshwater:** Only about **2.5% is freshwater**. Most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly recycled through the **hydrologic cycle**. It evaporates from the oceans into the sky, forms clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow, and flows through rivers back to the sea. The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nQuite simply, **life as we know it cannot exist without water.**\n* **Biology:** The human body is made of about **60% water**. Our brains are roughly 73% water, and our lungs are about 83%. \n* **Function:** Water regulates our internal body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and acts as the medium where all cellular chemical reactions take place.\n* **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of species, from microscopic plankton to the blue whale, the largest animal on Earth.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It sculpts the Earth's landscape, drives our weather systems, regulates global temperatures, and serves as the literal lifeblood of every living organism."
